A||||0||ezsurf||New Lower Ball Joints Worked!||||14||
Z||000000||ezsurf||11-22-2000||06:58 PM||||I mentioned some time ago that my 86 SE swayed at speeds above 60 mph.  I talked to Ed Parks and he said it was probably the lower rear ball joints.  He was right.  The car is still a little bit sensitive at higher speeds for my tastes, but the sway is gone.  I used McQuay-Norris (made in USA) ball joints that cost about $33 ea at Auto Zone.  I did have to make one modification though.  On the O.E. ball joint the grease boot comes from the bottom, the replacement joint had the boot on the top.  The boot needs to trimmed about 6mm all the way around.  This is because the boot is too long and gets jammed in the mounting socket.  If you leave one side of the boot a little longer (trim it back 4-5 mm instead of 6mm) the boot will cover the joint better.  These measurements are approximate.  I had to "eyeball it" as I assembled it.  At least now I have confidence the car will be a fun and useful driver.  I will also add that I did the test in the maint manual to see if I could detect play (slop) in the joints.  I could not detect ANY play whatsoever.  However, the joints definately needed to be replaced.  Now as soon as I get my rear sway bar installed, it ought to handle like a Ferrari!||||reg||14
